    Kernels {
      Kernel("CanvasKernel") {
        description: "Governs the canvas and its workspace: keeps the canvas free of the panel, its pixels literal, its looks honest about what they show, its edits checked, and fit the only door to the panel."
        metaphor: "The site foreman who checks that the wall is the wall and the window is only a window."
        directive_chains: ['/compose', '/look', '/inspect', '/edit', '/fit', '/bake', '/paint', 'ComposeFlow', 'LookFlow', 'InspectFlow', 'EditFlow', 'FitFlow', 'BakeFlow', 'DescentLoop']
        invariants: [
          "DESIGN ONLY: Nothing in this module is built. Its Functions graduate when the code lands: compose and fit into PixelFramePipeline, and the looks, inspection and edits into the coolled-verbs server, which has no layer spec yet.",
          "THE CANVAS IS NOT THE PANEL: A scene composes at its own size, and compose, the looks and inspection never consult a panel's geometry, palette threshold, upload budget or preview scale, so no look refuses a canvas for being too big to send.",
          "ONE DOOR TO THE PANEL: plan and accept see a canvas only through fit, and the panel preview is a look at fit's output, so what the preview shows and what a send writes cannot disagree.",
          "LITERAL PIXELS: Every lit pixel of a composed frame traces to one character of one sprite row. A placement may move, flip or recolour a sprite and a frame may show it, but nothing in a scene file computes pixels. A generator may propose pixels; once accepted, they belong to the artwork, not the generator.",
          "THE FILE IS THE ARTWORK: The scene file is the only state. A revision is the hash of its bytes, no server keeps a copy that matters, and a painter without the workspace can still edit the file by hand, which the next revision check notices.",
          "SCENES KEEP THEIR BYTES: A scene that names no size composes on the panel's canvas and fits whole, and compose ignores notes, note and block_in, so every scene written so far compiles to exactly its current packets and plan_id.",
          "A LOOK STATES ITS SCALE: Every picture is sized to the viewer's budget and says how many screen pixels each canvas pixel got and whether that is legible for this viewer. Rulers, grids and boxes are in canvas pixels, the numbers the file uses.",
          "A LOOK IS THE COMPOSITE: A region look crops the composed canvas with a margin, never a sprite or a cluster drawn alone, so whatever shows through a gap is in the look.",
          "EDITS ARE CHECKED: An edit lands only on the revision it was planned against, all of it or none of it, with its pixel diff shown and its inverse kept, so an edit that reaches beyond its region shows itself and can be undone.",
          "COPIES, NOT REFERENCES: An edit to one placement forks its sprite first, an edit to a sprite changes every copy and its diff shows each one, and a pattern from another scene arrives as a copy, so improving one thing never quietly repaints another.",
          "OVERLAYS ARE PINNED: An overlay's at is canvas pixels, not the feature it decorates, so changing what lies under it can strand it. A layer re-bakes only while it is a block-in, and every re-bake or edit names the overlays inside the boxes it changes.",
          "EVERY EDIT IS A REVISION: Each accepted edit makes a revision and each look names the revision it shows, so a diff, a trace and a log can always say what was seen.",
          "FIT REFUSES, NEVER TRUNCATES: A banner past max_columns, a pan past the frame budget, or a window off the canvas is refused before anything is composed, naming both numbers.",
          "NOTES ARE INERT: notes, note and block_in, brush provenance included, say why, how far and from where, for the next painter, agent or person. They never change a byte; fit only warns when block_in remains."
        ]
        tags: ["governance", "design-only"]
      }

      Kernel("FirstDrawingKernel") {
        description: "Governs the first drawing, so that it tests the loop and the workspace without building anything."
        metaphor: "The rules pinned inside the notebook's cover."
        directive_chains: ['/first_drawing', 'FirstDrawingFlow']
        invariants: [
          "NO PRODUCT CODE: No product code changes for the first drawing. Its workspace, brushes and records are not product code: after the run they are kept with the piece, in examples/canvas/ringed-planet, as the provenance its notes name.",
          "NO OTHER AGENTS YET: One painter, this agent, and the user as the judge. Fresh reviewers and comparisons wait until there is a workspace worth comparing.",
          "EVERY LOOK LOGGED: Each look's view and scale, what it showed and what it prompted are written down before the next look.",
          "BRUSHES BAKE: Any brush leaves rows in a new sprite with a note naming it, and the log says what was baked and what was painted by hand.",
          "ONE DRAWING IS AN ANECDOTE: It says which workspace pieces to build first, not that the loop works."
        ]
        tags: ["governance", "experiment"]
      }
    }
  }

  Semantics: {
    layer: "canvas -- design-only; would sit in front of PixelFramePipeline, with its workspace in the coolled-verbs server, and owns no runtime code yet"
    owns: |
      - The canvas: a scene composed at its own size, which may be far larger than any panel.
      - The workspace a painter perceives, navigates and edits a canvas through: views, provenance, and checked edits with pixel diffs.
      - Fitting, the one step that knows a panel's geometry and budgets.
      - Brushes, which run outside the file and propose rows, and forking a sprite before one copy changes.
      - The coarse-to-fine painting loop, and its first test.
    delegates: |
      - Stamping to the scene compositor (opensign.animation.scene), whose rules compose_canvas keeps exactly.
      - Panel frames to PixelFramePipeline, which bundles fit_still's and fit_pan's output as it bundles a scene today.
      - Banners to CoolLEDXProtocol's encode_text_banner, which already takes a bitmap of any width at panel height.
      - Sending, and writing edits, to coolled-verbs plan and accept; all wire encoding and every BLE write to CoolLEDXProtocol and GuardedTransport. Nothing here touches the radio.
    canvas_and_panel: |
      Today render does three things at once: it composes at the panel's size, works out what the panel would light, and returns a picture and text grids of it. The compositor is already size-free (compile_scene takes any width and height, and scripts/picture.py --width 512 renders a 512-wide scene), but every caller passes the panel's size, and picture.py refuses a still once one frame outgrows the 30 KiB upload: it renders 286x286 and refuses 287x287. This design splits them:
      - compose: scene to canvas, at the scene's size;
      - the workspace: canvas to what a painter needs to see and change, at the viewer's budget;
      - fit: canvas to what one panel can show, at the panel's size and budgets.
      The preview render returns today becomes a look at fit's output, which is why a scene that names no size keeps its packets and plan_id.
    workspace: |
      The artwork is the scene file; the workspace is how a painter perceives, navigates and edits it, and it never changes the artwork just to change the view. A painter needs the whole scene in mind no more than a programmer needs a whole codebase in mind to fix one function, and the parts line up with a programmer's tools:
      - Navigation: the overview for composition and rhythm, a region for detail, and the windows for what passers-by see.
      - The debugger: the outline, trace_pixel and hidden_placements, from a visible pixel to the placement and sprite cell that put it there, what it hides, and what lies buried.
      - Version control: plan_edit and apply_edit, with revisions, checked and reversible changes, and a pixel diff for each.
      - Refactoring: fork_sprite, so one copy changes without its siblings.
      - Code generators: brushes. The compiler: compose. The runtime: fit and the panel. The tests: the kernel's invariants.
      Seeing a mistake and diagnosing it are different steps. In the probe, the window reviewer saw the floating building but described it as a taller one; trace_pixel is what turns what a look shows into the line of the file that caused it. In the first drawing three traces settled every question a look raised and could not answer, and one showed that footprints the painter could not make out were there.
    scale: |
      - Nothing in a look's scale comes from the panel. 8 px per pixel is coolled-verbs' preview scale, chosen so a picture looks like LEDs, and it says nothing about what the painting loop needs.
      - Budget: about 1024 px on a picture's long side reaches the model unshrunk in Cursor, inferred from one picture: 4096x128 arrived at about 1024x32 on 2026-10-30.
      - Scale: a look takes the largest whole scale at which its area fits the budget, up to 16.
      - Legible: legible_px, the smallest scale at which a viewer still reads single pixels, belongs to the viewer and is measured, not assumed. The probe bracketed it between 2 and 8; the first drawing measured it for this agent: single pixels and dither patterns read cleanly at 4 px and not at 2, so legible_px is 3 or 4.
      - Leaf: a region is a leaf when its look is legible, so the leaf size is budget_px divided by legible_px, 256 pixels at 4 and 128 at 8. The overview's map grid uses that size, so each tile is one legible region look. The grid is a map, not the unit of work: of the first drawing's 17 region looks, 9 were tiles, taken to survey, and 8 were rectangles drawn round one subject (the lander, the trail, the storm's belt), taken to work.
      - Gridlines from 4 px up are a counting aid, not an imitation of LEDs, and rulers are labelled in canvas coordinates.
      - Text grids follow FeatureBoard card F9: none beside a picture unless asked. One 512x16 frame is 8,207 characters.
    time: |
      A canvas the panel scrolls or pans is also a sequence, hundreds of windows for a 512x16 banner, so it has two compositions: the whole as a picture, and the windows in the order they pass. A narrow gap that looks fine in the overview can leave a stretch of near-dark as it passes, and a panorama that is seamless as a picture need not be seamless as a loop. look_windows shows the sequence, the loop point and how lit each window is. It never replaces the overview; the local and the global have to inform each other.
    scene_format: |
      Four optional fields. Only size changes what compose does, and only when it is there:
      - "size": [width, height], the canvas. Without it, the canvas is the panel's.
      - "notes": {name: text} at the top level, the why of a sprite or of the whole piece.
      - "note" on a placement, the why of one copy, such as "lights staggered on purpose", or where it came from, such as a brush's name, version, seed and settings.
      - "block_in": true on a placement, a placeholder still waiting for paint, so the next painter can tell rough from finished.
      Nothing else joins the format: no shapes, groups, rules, recipes or references to other files. The scene stores the artwork, not a recipe that has to run forever to get the artwork back.
    fits: |
      Fit is where the panel's facts apply:
      - still: a panel-sized window of the canvas, sent as 0x03, or as 0x04 for a scene with frames. A canvas the panel's size fits whole, which is today's path.
      - banner: a canvas exactly the panel's height, scrolled by the panel's own firmware (0x02): smooth, a still, and no frame budget. A 512x16 canvas is 3,179 B in 25 packets, about 6 s to upload. The ceiling is about 5,100 columns if the 30 KiB limit governs 0x02 too.
      - pan: a window travelling a path, played as 0x04 within 79 frames at 64x16, each frame composing the scene's frame for its moment so a scene can animate as the window moves. A 64x128 canvas tilted top to bottom at 2 px per frame is 57 frames; panning 512 columns at 2 px is 225, and is refused.
      A square canvas reaches this panel only as a pan or as chosen windows, so it is painted for itself first and fitted after.
    brushes: |
      A generator may propose pixels, but once accepted they belong to the artwork, not the generator. A brush proposes rows, plan_edit shows them in place with their pixel diff, and apply_edit adds them as a new sprite and placement, only ever adding. The placement's note keeps the brush's name, version, seed and settings, which nothing needs to render but which tell the next painter where a shape came from.
      The first brushes: shapes (card F10's pie, circle, rectangle and line), seeded scatter (stars, snow, sand), ordered-dither ramps between two legend colours (a dawn sky, deep water), and exact patterns harvested from finished scenes, which arrive as copies. Pattern synthesis such as wave function collapse can come later, as one more texture brush invoked on purpose: it reproduces local neighbourhoods, not the intent of a composition, which is the staggered-windows objection again even when the rule is learned from an example. A split grammar for buildings would be another, laying down a starting texture rather than the result.
      In the first drawing re-baking a block-in where it stood (rebake_layer) was used more than adding, and safe only because nothing had been painted on top yet: an overlay is pinned to canvas pixels, so re-baking the planet with a new tilt would have left the storm floating off its belt. Finding what to regenerate is easy, since the notes name the brush and its settings; keeping the hand work attached is the hard part, and naming the overlays in the diff is the first answer. Baked rows were never edited by hand: the planet's rows are 225 characters wide, so the painter painted small sprites over them, and those overlays were the easiest things in the piece to move.
    recursion: |
      Recursion lives in four places, and only the first ever touches the file:
      - Composition: groups of sprites and groups, cut-outs of cut-outs, once a scene needs to move or recolour a cluster as one. Not yet.
      - Generation: brushes, outside the file, proposing rows a painter accepts.
      - Attention: the descent, a painter's loop over regions of the canvas.
      - Time: a scrolling or panning piece as a sequence of windows, judged as a sequence.
      Attention comes before syntax: the loop takes its regions from the canvas's geometry, not from structure in the file.
    descent: |
      paint(region):
          look at the region, with the rest of the scene as rough shapes
          block in or adjust, baking brushes into new sprites where hand-painting stops scaling
          where something looks wrong for no clear reason, trace it
          for each part worth refining: paint(part)
          look at the whole region again and mend the seams
      - A leaf is a region whose look is legible. A 64x16 canvas fits one look at 16 px per pixel, so for any viewer it is a leaf and the loop is today's render, look and fix.
      - Regions are rectangles, the overview's tiles or any box, not groups. A region look crops the composite, so whatever shows through a gap is in it, which a cluster drawn alone would hide, and a region may cut across three buildings and a meteor, which is the point.
      - Why regions at all: one look at a 512x16 strip found one of three planted defects, and the same strip in eight panel-sized windows found all three (evidence_basis).
    patterns: |
      What the first drawing's scripts kept doing, which the next pieces will need again:
      - One world model, shared: the planet's centre, the ring plane and the sun as a handful of constants every brush reads, so every shadow agrees and moving the sun is one line and a re-bake.
      - A brush is a function from a canvas pixel to a character, evaluated over a box and trimmed (bake_layer).
      - Dither anchored to the canvas: an ordered pattern keyed to canvas coordinates for smooth ramps (the terminator, the lamp pool), so layers line up; seeded grain for texture (the regolith), so nothing looks like a printer's screen.
      - Depth by splitting at an occluder: the rings as a back half and a front half around the planet, the night side and every shadow as layers of their own above what they darken.
      - K as opaque black, to hide what lies behind (the night side, the ground, shadows); '.' as see-through; coverage below full for translucency (the faint inner ring).
      - Shadows by ray tests against the shared model: the planet's on the rings and the rings' on the planet. Small shadows (the moonlet's, the lander's) were placed by hand from the same light.
      - The owner map: for every pixel, whose paint is on top and whose lies under it, behind trace, hidden, coverage and the overlay check.
      - Snapshots and diffs: a saved version before each batch of edits, and a diff proving the batch stayed where it was meant.
      - A stable file layout: one placement per line, one row per line, small hand-painted sprites before big baked ones, so edits land at known lines in a file too big to open.
      - A path with holds and easing, for showing a big canvas through the panel (fit_pan).
    ergonomics: |
      Clues from the first drawing's scratch work: each is something the painter did by hand around the tools, which the surface should do instead.
      - Every batch of edits was followed by a hand-made snapshot, twelve in all: an accepted edit should be a revision by itself, and every look should name the revision it shows.
      - Looks were numbered in their file names so the log could point at them: the server should keep a journal of looks and edits (journal_look), so the painter's log only needs the why.
      - Every trace asked about three or four pixels, through a shell loop that once broke on word splitting, and stripped the notes with sed: trace takes many pixels and leaves notes out by default.
      - Every diff was read for its first line, the count and the boxes; the picture came second.
      - The four ad-hoc edit scripts removed sprites, removed buried placements and cleared a flag on every placement; the ten text edits inserted placements before a named one, moved placements and replaced two rows of a sprite. Those are the first edit operations, ahead of cell edits.
      - One text edit failed because a brush had rewritten the file's layout: tools that write the file keep its layout stable, and edits that go through operations stop depending on its text at all.
      - Brush output was silenced except for small sprites: a bake should come back as a picture of the result in place, with its diff, and rows only on request.
      - The one call to coolled-verbs, planning the tour, came back as eight pictures of the same frame because the tour opens on a hold: a long bundle's preview should sample frames across the loop.
      - A stats script, a clean render and a side-by-side of three versions were each written once: coverage, a view without grid or rulers, and a comparison of revisions belong in the surface.
      - Region looks were tiles to survey and rectangles to work: region takes any rectangle.
    surface: |
      coolled-verbs went unused for the whole first drawing: it composes only at the panel's size, takes a scene inline, and returns one fixed-scale picture. The one call that worked, at the very end, went through the bundle noun, which takes a path. Keeping its four verbs, the drawing says the surface should be:
      - Everything by path. A scene file is the reference and a revision is the hash of its bytes; inline scenes stay for panel-sized sketches (FeatureBoard card F12).
      - render scene with a view: panel, today's output and the default for a scene with no size; overview; region, any rectangle with a margin; windows; or clean, with no grid or rulers, for sharing. revision looks at a past one, and compare puts revisions side by side. Each look comes back as one picture and one line of text: extent, scale, ruler step, revision and journal number. No text grids beside pictures (card F9).
      - describe scene: the revision, size and counts; with at, a list of pixels, trace_pixel; with hidden, hidden_placements; coverage; outline for a region; history, the journal.
      - plan edit with operations against a revision, then accept: the plan leads with the count and boxes, then the picture, the forks it made and the overlays inside the changed boxes; accept writes the file and returns the new revision. A re-bake is a plan edit that replaces a block-in's rows.
      - plan scene with show (still, banner, or pan with a path, step, holds and easing), then accept to send. Its preview samples frames across the loop.
      - Brushes stay scripts, outside the file and outside the server. They write the scene file like any other editor, and the next revision check notices.
      - Three authorities stay separate: looking and planning change nothing; accepting an edit writes a file, behind a gate of its own; accepting a send drives the panel, behind --execute.
      What to build first, in order: trace and hidden, then diff, then rendering at the scene's own size with overview and region, then the edit operations, then re-baking. The outline and revision checks between painters wait for a second painter.
    first_drawing: |
      The first experiment needs no other agents: the user asks this agent to paint a scene of its choice, and judges it.
      - Canvas: the painter's choice, large enough that its first overview cannot show single pixels. At a 1024 px budget that is at least 384 on the long side, where the overview falls to 2 px per pixel and the probe's single-pixel defects went unseen. If the first overview reads every pixel, the canvas is too small, so go bigger.
      - Workspace: a throwaway prototype outside the repo (prototype_workspace) with an overview in leaf-sized tiles, region looks, pixel trace, hidden placements and a diff between saved versions. Edits are the painter's own file edits, and brushes, if any, bake rows with notes.
      - Records, outside the repo: plan.txt before the first look; log.txt after every look; the scene file; account.txt.
      - What to read from it: the user's judgement of the piece as a whole and up close. From the log: what region looks caught that the overview did not, what was seen but not understood until traced, which seams needed mending, and how much of the scene file had to be read at once. From the account: the smallest scale at which single pixels still read, the first reading of legible_px.
      - What it decides: which workspace pieces to build first in coolled-verbs (size, overview and region, trace, checked edits), with cards F9 and F12.
      - The piece can reach the panel today as a tour: up to 79 panel windows along a path, composed by the workspace, saved as a frame bundle and sent with plan bundle and accept, with no product code.
      Ran on 2026-10-31 and kept in examples/canvas/ringed-planet with its tools and records: the view from a small moon, a ringed gas giant rising over its horizon, 384x384, 257 placements of 34 sprites, in 25 looks (8 of the whole, 17 of regions). What it found:
      - Every coarse problem was light, and none looked like a lighting problem: the sun moved three times, each move found by tracing a muddy wedge or a missing stretch of ring back to a shadow.
      - Every finer flaw surfaced in a region look and none in the overview: a dot-screen ground, a lamp pool swallowing the lander, windows lost in the gold foil, ring gaps aliasing into specks, a storm placed 6 px off its belt.
      - legible_px is 3 or 4 for this agent, so the leaf at a 1024 px budget is about 256 pixels.
      - 98.2% of the lit pixels came from brushes, 1.3% from 21 hand-painted sprites and 0.6% from scattered stars; the hand-painted part carries most of the story, and no baked row was edited by hand.
      - The tools, by use: 17 region looks, 7 overviews, 12 snapshots, 4 diffs, 3 traces of 11 pixels, 2 hidden checks (24 buried stars found, then none left), no outline. The 246 KB scene file was never opened whole.
    later_trials: |
      Once the workspace exists, trials with fresh agents can compare it fairly:
      - Three conditions on the same planted defects: the whole only; overview and overlapping windows; overview, windows, trace and checked edits. Measure detection, correct diagnosis, successful repairs, unintended changes, tool calls and tokens, over several defect placements and reviewers.
      - Continuation: a different model improves one region of a finished piece from the file, its notes and the workspace alone, and the whole is judged for coherence.
      - Calibration: the picture budget and legible_px measured with fresh agents on pictures of known detail, instead of self-reported.
    relation_to_feature_board: |
      - F9 (lean results) and F12 (scene files) come first: at canvas sizes, text grids and re-sent scenes are the cost. The 512x16 probe scene was 8,063 characters, re-sent with every inline render.
      - F10 changes shape: as written, a pie is a placement drawn at compile time, which breaks LITERAL PIXELS and would freeze its rasterizer into every saved scene's bytes. Here it is a brush that bakes a sprite.
      - F11 becomes a fit: the pan that enters one edge and leaves the other.
      - F6, F7, F8 and F13 keep their shape: they say where, when and which way round, never what.
      - F13's K already works today, as a letter outside the legend recoloured black: the first drawing used it for the night side, the ground and every shadow. What the card adds is making it the default.
    evidence_basis: |
      - 2026-10-30 probe, run outside the repo: the night skyline tiled eight times into a 512x16 scene of 153 placements, with three planted defects (a building floating 3 px up, a moon shaped like a C, a one-pixel slit down a tower). A fresh agent shown the whole strip as one picture, 4096x128 at 8 px per pixel, which reached it at about 2 px per pixel, found one and sensed a second. A fresh agent shown the same strip as eight 64-column windows found all three at the right columns and called the piece tiled, but described the floating building as a taller one: it saw the defect without diagnosing it.
      - The same scene was 8,063 characters of JSON and 8,207 characters of text grid per frame; as an 0x02 banner it is 3,179 B in 25 packets, about 6 s at the panel's measured 0.236 s per packet.
      - compile_scene composes at any size; coolled-verbs passes the profile's 64x16; scripts/picture.py rendered the probe scene at 512x16 and at 286x286, and refused 287x287 with "1 frames is more than the 0 one upload holds".
      - Panning 512 columns at 2 px per frame is 225 0x04 frames, against a budget of 79.
      - 2026-10-31 first drawing (examples/canvas/ringed-planet, with plan.txt, log.txt, account.txt, every look and every snapshot): single pixels read at 4 px and not at 2; 98.2% of lit pixels baked, 1.3% hand-painted, 0.6% scattered; hidden_placements found 24 stars buried behind the opaque rings; a diff of only 108 pixels flagged the storm's miss, and the next look's rulers placed it; the tour's plan preview was eight copies of its opening hold.
    open_questions: |
      - What is legible_px for other viewers, and is 1024 px the budget for an MCP tool's pictures too, or only for pictures read from files? The first drawing measured 3 or 4 for this agent, from pictures read from files; a calibration with fresh agents settles it.
      - Should an overlay be able to say what it sits on, so a re-bake can carry it along, or is naming it in the diff enough? The second keeps the file literal.
      - Does the journal belong to the server's session, or in the scene's folder beside its log, where the next painter would find it?
      - Which files may plan edit write? accept's --execute gate is about the radio, so edits need a gate of their own, such as one workspace directory.
      - Where does undo live: inverses kept by the server, which forgets them on restart, or the file's own history in git?
      - Should a canvas meant for another display get its own palette, or stay in the panel's 8-colour legend, which is what keeps rows readable?
      - Does the panel's own scroll join a banner's last column to its first, or leave a gap? look_windows assumes it joins.
      - Should a region look's margin show its colours plainly behind a marked edge, as designed, or dimmed, which the 8-colour legend makes ambiguous?
    provenance: |
      - Came out of the agent-drawing session and the follow-up on groups and harvesting (2026-10-30): painting coarse to fine like a painter, recursion in the loop rather than the file, generators kept outside the file as brushes, names local to each scene, and shared things arriving as copies.
      - The 512x16 probe the same night measured where one look stops working, and the split into compose, workspace and fit followed from it.
      - A review of both accounts the same night named the workspace as its own layer, separated seeing a defect from diagnosing it, asked for pixel provenance and for revision-checked edits with pixel diffs, and for scrolling pieces to be judged as a sequence. The first drawing became one painter, this agent, instead of a comparison between fresh agents, and the 8 px preview scale stopped standing in for what the loop needs.
      - The first drawing ran on 2026-10-31. Its scripts were kept with it, on the user's view that they are part of the artwork, and what the painter did by hand around them became the patterns, the ergonomics and the revised surface above.
  }
}
